<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Red River (Asia)</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Sông Hồng</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Red River rises in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunnan">Yunnan</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and flows south-east through northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> to the Gulf of Tonkin. Its fertile, flood-prone delta, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanoi">Hanoi</a> stands, was the heartland of the earliest Vietnamese states and of later dynasties, which built great dikes to control it. French expeditions up the river in the 1870s and 1880s led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> of Tonkin.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/red-river-asia.jpg" alt="Red River (Asia)" width="320" height="264" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kmusser · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Redriverasiamap.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
